#Title sunlight on shallow water
#Composer SuperForge (procedural)
#Tempo 56

; The lakeside rail's own piece, and the FOURTH in the tree. The other three
; are spoken for: `slice_b_song` is the room rail's ambient bed, whose composed
; rest half-bar is the window its reverb A/B is measured in; `drive_song` is
; D minor and written for a shooter; `circuit_song` is A mixolydian and written
; for a road. A lake in the afternoon wants none of those. This one is WARM AND
; UNHURRIED: F major, mid-tempo, and it never lands hard on the tonic.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE HARMONY, AND WHY IT IS SEVENTHS AND NINTHS RATHER THAN TRIADS
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Eight bars, played twice:
;
;   | Fmaj7   | Fmaj7   | Dm9     | Dm9     |
;   | B(b)maj9| B(b)maj9| Gm7     | C7sus4  |
;
; That is I - vi - IV - ii - V, the turnaround every warm afternoon is built
; on, with two deliberate refusals in it:
;
;   * NO PLAIN TRIADS. Every chord carries its seventh and most carry a ninth.
;     A triad states a key; a maj7 with a ninth on top states a COLOUR and
;     leaves the key slightly open, which is the difference between "F major"
;     and "sunlight". The pad on channel B voices the two GUIDE TONES (third
;     and seventh) and nothing else, so the colour is carried by the interval
;     rather than by stacking every note of the chord.
;   * THE V IS SUSPENDED AND STAYS THAT WAY. C7sus4 has an F where its third
;     would be, so the leading tone (E) is simply absent from the dominant and
;     the pull back to F is a lean rather than a snap. The lead's last bar
;     settles onto G — the fifth of that sus — and the loop turns over
;     underneath it. Nothing here ever resolves hard; that is the piece.
;
; F major's one flat is written as the key signature, so `b` reads as B(b)
; everywhere below and no bar has to spell it.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE ECHO
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 96 ms of buffer (12 KiB of ARAM) behind a low-passed FIR: a soft, wide tail
; that darkens rather than a slap. Taps sum to 128, which is the compiler's
; limit exactly.
;
; EVOL AND EFB ARE NOT THIS SONG'S TO CHOOSE. 12/24 is a tree-wide constant:
; `room_a_ambience` and `beam_end` write those two numbers back as LITERALS
; when they finish, so a song whose header disagrees leaves any rail using
; those effects in a rest state it was never in, permanently, after the first
; use. drive_song learned that from a red landing gate and circuit_song copied
; the lesson; this is the third file to carry it. The buffer length and the FIR
; are mine, because nothing writes those back — the vendored compiler
; structurally refuses `set_echo_delay` inside a sound effect, and no effect in
; the tree touches the FIR taps.
#EchoLength 96
#EchoVolume 12
#EchoFeedback 24
#FirFilter 84 28 12 4 0 0 0 0

#KeySignature F

@1 pluck
@2 square_lead
@3 tri_bass
@4 step
@5 kick
@6 bell

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE KIT DOES NOT TOUCH THE NOISE GENERATOR. There is exactly one, and the
; driver zeroes the volume of every music channel sharing it the moment a sound
; effect wants it (audio-driver.asm, the `SfxNoise` branch). This rail's own
; wave cue IS a noise effect and it fires every couple of seconds, so a noise
; hi-hat here would drop out on a schedule — the worst version of the bug,
; because it would sound deliberate. The kit is samples: `kick` on channel F,
; and `step` at o5 for the hat and o3 for the snare, exactly as the other two
; songs do it.
;
; THE GROOVE IS A SHUFFLE AND A BACK-BEAT. Each beat is 24 ticks split 16 + 8,
; which is a swung eighth (2:1) rather than two straight twelves — the whole
; difference between "unhurried" and "slow". The snare takes the LONG half of
; beats 2 and 4 and the hat keeps the short halves, so the accent lands behind
; the beat instead of on it. The kick alternates two bars and is deliberately
; NOT four-on-the-floor: bar one puts it on 1 and the and-of-3, bar two on 1
; and 3, which leaves beat 4 to the snare alone.
;
; K0 disables key-off on both drum channels: these are one-shots that decay to
; zero on their own and a key-off would choke the kick's tail. Gaps are `w`
; waits, never `r` rests — a rest still keys off under K0.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!hl v5  o5 c%16          ; the long half of the shuffle
!hs v4  o5 c%8           ; ...and the short one, quieter
!sn v12 o3 g%16          ; the back-beat, where a hat's long half would be
!e  !hl !hs !sn !hs !hl !hs !sn !hs
!k1 o3 c%24 w%36 c%12 w%24        ; 1 and the and-of-3
!k2 o3 c%24 w%24 c%24 w%24        ; 1 and 3

; ---- A: the lead. Pluck, wet, quiet, and it sits ON TOP of the bed rather
;         than over it -- `pluck` is a one-shot that decays from its own
;         key-on, so this voice physically cannot shout. It holds off for the
;         first two bars so the groove and the pad state themselves, then
;         answers across the changes.
;
; EVERY BAR CARRIES ITS OWN OCTAVE, so a `>` inside one cannot leak into the
; next -- the class of bug that is silent in MML and obvious on the chip. The
; notes are chord tones or the ninth of the bar they sit in; `pluck` tops out
; at o4 (its `last_octave` in slice_b.terrificaudio), which is why the second
; pass climbs to F4 and no further.
;
; The vibrato is delayed by an eighth, so it only reaches the long notes and
; the eighth-note runs stay straight.
A  L @1 v10 E1 Q7 MP10,l16,l8 l8
A  r1                                | r1
A  o3 a4 >c8 d8 e4. d8               | o4 d4 c4 <a2
A  o4 d4. c8 <b4 a4                  | o4 c4 <a4 >d4 c4
A  o3 b4. >d8 <b4 a4                 | o4 c4 <b8 a8 g2
; ---- and the second pass, which sings all eight bars
A  o3 a4. >c8 e4 d8 c8               | o4 c4 d8 c8 <a2
A  o4 e4. d8 c4 <a4                  | o4 d4 e8 d8 c2
A  o4 f4. e8 d4 c4                   | o4 d2 c4 <a4
A  o4 d4 c8 <b8 a4 g4                | o4 c2 <g2

; ---- B: the pad. GUIDE TONES ONLY -- the third and the seventh of each
;         chord, one per half bar, and nothing else. Two notes name a seventh
;         chord unambiguously and leave the root to the bass and the ninth to
;         the bell, so three quiet voices spell a five-note chord between them
;         and no single channel has to be loud. Pulse, wet, panned left.
;
; It carries the FIRST pass and rests through the second, which is where the
; bell takes over: with every part sounding for all sixteen bars the loop has
; no shape, and the two halves are otherwise distinguished only by their
; register.
B  L @2 v6 E1 p46 l2
B  o3 a2 >e2                         | o3 a2 >e2
B  o3 f2 >c2                         | o3 f2 >c2
B  o3 d2 a2                          | o3 d2 a2
B  o3 b2 >f2                         | o3 f2 b2
B  r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1

; ---- C: the bass. Written out bar by bar rather than transposed, and that is
;         the point of it: a transposed figure preserves INTERVALS, so it can
;         only ever use root, fifth, octave and ninth -- the degrees that mean
;         the same over a major and a minor chord. This line wants the thirds
;         and the approach notes as well. Every bar therefore ends on a note
;         that LEADS to the next root: bar 2 on A into D, bar 4 on A into B(b)
;         a semitone up, bar 6 on A into G, bar 8 on E into F a semitone up.
;         Dry, and the only part that plays all sixteen bars.
C  L @3 v12 E0 Q6 l8
C  o2 f8 >c8 <f8 a8 >c8 <a8 f8 g8    | o2 f8 >c8 <f8 >c8 <a8 f8 g8 a8
C  o2 d8 a8 >d8 <a8 f8 a8 d8 e8      | o2 d8 a8 >d8 <f8 a8 d8 e8 a8
C  o2 b8 >f8 <b8 >d8 <b8 >f8 d8 c8   | o2 b8 >f8 <b8 >c8 d8 c8 <b8 a8
C  o2 g8 >d8 <g8 b8 >d8 <b8 g8 a8    | o3 c8 <g8 >c8 <b8 g8 >c8 <b8 e8
C  o2 f8 >c8 <f8 a8 >c8 <a8 f8 g8    | o2 f8 >c8 <f8 >c8 <a8 f8 g8 a8
C  o2 d8 a8 >d8 <a8 f8 a8 d8 e8      | o2 d8 a8 >d8 <f8 a8 d8 e8 a8
C  o2 b8 >f8 <b8 >d8 <b8 >f8 d8 c8   | o2 b8 >f8 <b8 >c8 d8 c8 <b8 a8
C  o2 g8 >d8 <g8 b8 >d8 <b8 g8 a8    | o3 c8 <g8 >c8 <b8 g8 >c8 <b8 e8

; ---- D: the sparkle. A broken chord per bar at a 32nd, bell, very quiet,
;         panned right, wet. It fills the SECOND pass and leaves the first to
;         the pad -- the same split from the other side. Its three pitches are
;         the ninth and the upper extensions the pad deliberately does not
;         voice, which is where the add-nine colour actually comes from.
D  L @6 v5 E1 p92 o4
D  r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1
D  {{ceg}}1,%3 {{ceg}}1,%3
D  {{cea}}1,%3 {{cea}}1,%3
D  {{dfa}}1,%3 {{dfa}}1,%3
D  {{dfb}}1,%3 {{fgb}}1,%3

; ---- E: hat and snare.   ---- F: kick.
; Nothing is written to G or H: those are the two channels the driver ducks
; while a sound effect plays, and on THIS rail an effect plays every couple of
; seconds. A part scored there would not merely be at risk; it would be gone
; on a schedule.
E  L @4 K0 E0
E  [!e ]16
F  L @5 v15 K0 E0
F  [!k1 !k2 ]8
